<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired"><h2>What it inspired</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Mary Gently Laid Her Child (Joseph S. Cook)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">same melody</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Songs without Words (William Lloyd Webber)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">the carol included as one of the pieces</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Christmas Greetings (Bing Crosby)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1949</span><span class="sg-chain-h">recorded</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Christmas Jazz (Trans-Siberian Orchestra, on The Lost Christmas Eve)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2004</span><span class="sg-chain-h">the tune reworked</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Good Swing Wenceslas (Count Basie Orchestra, arr. Sammy Nestico)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2015</span><span class="sg-chain-h">big band arrangement</span></li></ul></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired-by"><h2>What it drew on</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Tempus adest floridum</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">13th century; first printed 1582</span><span class="sg-chain-h">same melody</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Sanct Wenceslaw und Podiwin (Václav Alois Svoboda)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1847</span><span class="sg-chain-h">poem that gave the background material</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Deeds of Faith: Stories for Children from Church History (John Mason Neale)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1849</span><span class="sg-chain-h">Neale's earlier prose telling of the legend, adapted into the carol</span></li></ul></section>
